from codex_support import *
from lessonlib import num
F = fact

def unit(n, title, overview, application, method, facts, **kw):
    concept_lesson("logic", "l3-", n, title, overview, application, method, facts, "intro-logic", **kw)

unit(1,"Basic notions of logic",
 "Logic studies how reasons support conclusions. An argument is not merely a quarrel: it contains claims offered as reasons and a claim those reasons are meant to support. Reasons are premises; the claim supported is the conclusion. An inference is the step from premises to conclusion. First identify what a speaker is trying to establish, then ask whether the reasons actually support it. A confident voice, popularity or repetition does not establish that an inference is good.",
 "At a Bamenda school, Bih argues that the library should open after lessons because pupils need a quiet place to read. The proposed opening is the conclusion; the need for quiet reading space is a reason. Another pupil might agree with the conclusion while questioning whether staffing makes the plan possible. Separate the proposal from its support before judging it.",
 "Underline a proposed conclusion and list the reasons separately. Words such as because and therefore may help, but they are clues rather than guarantees. Ask whether a sentence is giving support or receiving support. A collection of unrelated statements is not automatically an argument.",[
 F("argument","claims offered as reasons for a conclusion","Ayuk says the path is muddy, so the class should use the paved route.","An argument must be an angry quarrel.","A calm proposal with supporting reasons is an argument; anger is irrelevant."),
 F("premise","a claim offered as support","Bih says: the classroom roof leaks; therefore the books need moving. Consider the leaking roof claim.","A premise is always the final claim being defended.","The leaking roof is a reason; moving the books is the conclusion."),
 F("conclusion","the claim the reasons are intended to establish","Nji says the clinic is nearer, so we should go there. Consider we should go there.","A conclusion is necessarily the first sentence.","Its role as the supported claim matters, not its position."),
 F("inference","the reasoning step from premises to a conclusion","A learner moves from all club members have badges and Bih is a member to Bih has a badge.","An inference is just copying an unrelated sentence.","An inference links support to a conclusion; it is not mere copying.")])

unit(2,"The value of logic",
 "Logic helps us judge reasons, communicate clearly and revise beliefs when evidence changes. It does not make every belief correct or remove the need for factual investigation. Relevant evidence addresses the claim at issue. Consistent claims can hold together without contradiction in the same circumstances. A fallacy is a mistake in reasoning. The value of a conclusion should be assessed through the reasons, rather than through the speaker's status or the popularity of the claim.",
 "A Douala club must choose whether to repair its reading room. A report on the leaking roof is relevant. The colour of the chairperson's shirt is not. The club can ask for evidence without attacking the speaker. If a measurement was wrong, admitting and correcting it improves the discussion rather than showing defeat.",
 "Ask what would count as evidence and whether the offered reason concerns that issue. Check whether claims conflict under the same conditions. Challenge an inference politely; do not confuse criticism of a reason with an insult to its author. A good process can still begin with incomplete information, so remain open to correction.",[
 F("relevance","a reason's bearing on the claim under discussion","A proposal to repair a roof cites water entering through holes.","Any interesting fact supports any conclusion.","The evidence must bear on the particular conclusion; interest alone is insufficient."),
 F("consistency","claims being able to hold together without contradiction in the same respect","The club says its door is open this afternoon and not locked this afternoon.","Consistency means repeating words loudly.","Consistency concerns compatibility of claims, not loudness or repetition."),
 F("evidence","information used to support or test a factual claim","A learner checks a rain gauge before reporting rainfall.","A rumour is conclusive evidence because many people repeat it.","Repetition does not establish reliability; examine the underlying observations."),
 F("fallacy","a mistake in the reasoning offered","A learner rejects a rainfall report solely because the reporter has old shoes.","Disliking a reporter disproves the report.","The reporter's shoes do not establish whether the rainfall report is correct.")])

unit(3,"Divisions of logic",
 "A traditional school distinction separates formal logic, which examines the structure of reasoning, from material or applied assessment, which examines its content and truth. Other classifications distinguish deductive and inductive reasoning. These classifications answer different questions and should not be treated as identical. A valid deductive form preserves truth if its premises are true; checking that form does not establish the truth of the premises. Investigating a claim about a local market still requires evidence.",
 "Suppose all members of a hypothetical Buea club have cards and Ayuk is a member. The form supports the conclusion that Ayuk has a card. Whether that club really issued cards is a separate factual question. A survey predicting attendance from previous meetings makes another kind of move: it offers probable, not guaranteed, support.",
 "Ask first whether the exercise concerns form, factual content or strength of inference. Do not label an argument false merely because its form is invalid: truth applies to statements, while validity applies to deductive reasoning. Traditional labels vary between textbooks; compare the reasoning task as well as the name.",[
 F("formal logic","assessment of the structure of an inference","A learner checks the pattern all members have cards; Bih is a member; therefore Bih has a card.","Formal validity proves that every premise is factually true.","A valid pattern preserves truth conditionally; premise truth needs separate checking."),
 F("material assessment","examination of the content or factual truth of claims","A learner checks the actual club register to see whether Bih belongs.","Checking a register is only an examination of sentence shape.","Consulting a register checks content, not just logical form."),
 F("deductive assessment","asking whether true premises would guarantee the conclusion","A learner asks if a counterexample could leave the premises true but the conclusion false.","Any plausible conclusion is deductively valid.","Deductive validity excludes a true-premise, false-conclusion counterexample."),
 F("inductive assessment","judging how strongly evidence supports a probable conclusion","A learner evaluates how representative a market survey is before generalising.","A sample guarantees every future case.","An induction can support a probability without guaranteeing all cases.")])

unit(4,"Types of logic and reasoning",
 "Deductive reasoning aims at a conclusion guaranteed by its premises when the form is valid. Inductive reasoning offers support of a degree: a larger, more representative sample can improve it without making the conclusion certain. Symbolic logic represents relationships with signs; informal analysis examines arguments in ordinary language. Symbolic and informal are ways of presenting or examining reasoning, not synonyms for deductive and inductive. A claim can be symbolised without becoming true.",
 "Ayuk checks a club rule that applies to every member and then applies it to a named member: this can be a deduction. Ngozi samples several market stalls to estimate a wider pattern: this is induction. Either argument can be written in ordinary sentences, and some relationships can also be represented symbolically.",
 "Identify the kind of support being claimed. For a deduction, try to imagine true premises with a false conclusion. For an induction, ask about sample size, selection and alternative explanations. Do not mistake a sequence of impressive symbols for reliable evidence.",[
 F("deduction","reasoning that aims to guarantee its conclusion from its premises","All cards in a hypothetical box are blue; this card is from that box; therefore it is blue.","Deduction is defined simply as moving from many cases to a generalisation.","The defining aim is necessary support, not the number of cases mentioned."),
 F("induction","reasoning that supports a conclusion as probable from evidence","Several sampled stalls sell plantains, so a learner predicts that many stalls in the market do.","Induction guarantees the unexamined stalls.","Unexamined cases may differ, so the support is probable."),
 F("symbolic representation","use of symbols to express logical relationships","A pupil writes P and not P to display a conflict in a claim.","A symbolic expression is true merely because it uses letters.","Symbols display relationships; they do not verify facts."),
 F("informal analysis","assessment of reasoning expressed in ordinary language","A class checks whether a club proposal's stated reasons address the proposal.","Informal analysis means accepting careless reasoning.","Ordinary language can be assessed carefully and rigorously.")])

truth = num("l3-6-truth",2,
 "In classical logic let P have value {p} and Q have value {q}, where {1} means true and {0} means false. Give the truth value of P AND Q.",
 "p && q ? 1 : 0",["AND is true only when both statements are true.","P has value {p}; Q has value {q}; the result is {answer}."],
 vars={"p":{"int":[0,1]},"q":{"int":[0,1]}},
 misc=[("or","p || q ? 1 : 0","That treats AND as OR; AND requires both statements to be true.","p != q"),
       ("opposite","p && q ? 0 : 1","That reverses the truth value; test whether both inputs are true.")],sources=["intro-logic"])
unit(6,"Laws of thought in classical logic",
 "Classical logic uses the laws of identity, non-contradiction and excluded middle. Identity keeps a thing or proposition itself: P is P. Non-contradiction says P and not P cannot both be true at the same time and in the same respect. Excluded middle says P or not P. It does not say that we know which is true. Changes of time or meaning can remove an apparent contradiction, so always state the conditions precisely. Other logical systems exist; this lesson uses the classical school framework.",
 "A Buea classroom door can be open in the morning and closed at night without contradiction. Claiming that it is fully open and not open at the same instant, using open in the same way, is different. Calling a business bank a river bank changes meaning; it cannot support an argument that depends on keeping bank fixed.",
 "Write one proposition clearly before negating it. Keep its subject, time and meaning fixed. Distinguish a contradiction from a mere difference or lack of knowledge. In a computed truth exercise, evaluate the relationship rather than guessing from the number of words.",[
 F("identity","keeping a thing or proposition identical with itself in an argument","A class uses library to mean the same reading room throughout its discussion.","A term may change meaning unnoticed halfway through a proof.","A valid comparison keeps the relevant meaning fixed."),
 F("non-contradiction","excluding P and not P as both true in the same respect and time","A notice cannot truthfully say the same room is fully open and not open at the same instant.","Open in the morning and closed at night is a contradiction.","The different times remove the conflict; fix time and respect before applying the law."),
 F("excluded middle","the classical rule P or not P","Either the specified library light is on now or it is not on now.","Not knowing whether a light is on defeats excluded middle.","The law concerns the alternatives, not our knowledge of which holds."),
 F("qualification","specifying the time and respect of a claim","A learner distinguishes a heavy bag from a heavy school workload before comparing claims.","Every use of the same word has exactly the same meaning.","Words can be used in different senses; specify the relevant sense.")],replacement={3:truth})

unit(7,"The significance of the laws of thought",
 "The classical laws help keep reasoning stable and detect incompatible claims. Their use requires care with meaning, time and conditions. Identity discourages equivocation: using one word with different meanings as if it were unchanged. Non-contradiction exposes claims that cannot hold together under the same conditions. Excluded middle concerns P and its exact negation, not any pair of opposite-sounding words. Unknown does not mean false; neither does disagreement by itself settle the facts.",
 "A notice says the Bamenda club meeting is cancelled. Another says it is not cancelled for the same meeting at the same time. The class should check the date and source rather than accept both unchanged. If a second notice concerns next week's meeting, the statements may be compatible. Precision prevents unnecessary disputes.",
 "Restate both claims using the same terms. Match their times and subjects. Ask whether one is genuinely the negation of the other. If information is missing, suspend judgment and investigate. Logical rules organise evidence; they do not supply the missing timetable or observe the meeting for us.",[
 F("equivocation","switching a word's meaning within reasoning","A learner moves from saving at a bank to sitting on a river bank as though bank meant the same thing.","Repeating the same spelling guarantees the same meaning.","One spelling can have different meanings; this switch breaks the reasoning."),
 F("contradictory claims","claims that negate one another under the same conditions","The same meeting is cancelled and the same meeting is not cancelled.","Any disagreement about different meetings is a contradiction.","A genuine contradiction needs the same meeting and conditions."),
 F("exact negation","denial of the same proposition rather than a different claim","The denial of the room is open now is the room is not open now.","The denial of the room is open is the room is large.","Large concerns size, not whether the room is open."),
 F("suspended judgment","withholding a decision when evidence is insufficient","Bih has not checked the notice and says she does not yet know if the meeting is cancelled.","Uncertainty proves the meeting is not cancelled.","Lack of knowledge establishes neither alternative; investigate.")],prerequisites=[6])

unit(8,"Operations of the mind",
 "In the traditional school account, simple apprehension forms or grasps a concept without yet affirming or denying anything. Judgment affirms or denies a relation and is expressed in a proposition. Reasoning connects judgments so that one is supported by others. A term expresses a concept; a proposition can be true or false; an argument contains an inference. These operations are linked but are not interchangeable. Thinking of market alone does not assert that a market is open.",
 "Ngozi thinks about a market: she grasps an idea. She then judges that the market is open today. Finally she reasons that traders can enter because the market is open and entry is permitted. The last move needs premises and a conclusion. Each stage adds a different logical role, rather than simply a longer phrase.",
 "Ask whether the example merely names something, asserts something or supports a claim from other claims. Do not classify every sentence as reasoning: a bare assertion gives no supporting inference. The traditional terminology is the course framework, not a claim that psychologists classify every thought this way.",[
 F("simple apprehension","grasping a concept without affirming or denying","A learner thinks of a library without claiming that it is open.","Merely thinking library asserts that every library is open.","The concept alone makes no affirmation about opening."),
 F("judgment","affirming or denying a relation","A learner asserts that the library is open today.","Every judgment already includes a supporting argument.","A judgment can be asserted without giving its reasons."),
 F("reasoning","drawing a supported judgment from other judgments","The rules allow entry when the library opens; it is open; so entry is allowed.","A single unsupported assertion is automatically an inference.","Reasoning requires a movement from supporting claims to a conclusion."),
 F("term","a linguistic expression of a concept","The word library expresses an idea in a discussion.","An isolated term must be true or false.","Truth and falsity apply to assertions, not an isolated term in this use.")])

unit(9,"Ideas and terms",
 "An idea or concept is what we grasp mentally; a term is its expression in language. A term can contain several words, such as school library. In traditional terminology, intension or comprehension concerns the defining attributes of a concept, while extension concerns the things to which it applies. A more specific concept can have more defining attributes but fewer instances. Keep the meanings fixed when making that comparison; do not confuse the word's length with its logical role.",
 "Library and school library can be compared in a Buea discussion. School library adds a qualifying attribute and applies to a narrower class of libraries. The expression contains several words but functions as one term. Counting letters would not tell us which objects fall within its extension.",
 "Separate the expression, its meaning and the instances it covers. Give an example and a non-example when testing a proposed definition. Narrowing a concept is not the same as proving that a particular object exists. Teachers should confirm the course's preferred labels comprehension and extension.",[
 F("concept","an idea grasped by the mind","A learner understands what a library is before choosing a word for it.","An idea and the ink used to write a word are identical objects.","The idea is its meaning, not the physical ink or sound."),
 F("term","a word or group of words expressing a concept","School library functions as one expression in a classification.","A term must contain only a single word.","Several words can express one concept and function as a term."),
 F("intension","the attributes included in a concept","A school library adds belonging to a school to the general idea of a library.","Intension is the number of letters in the term.","Intension concerns attributes, not spelling length."),
 F("extension","the things to which a concept applies","A learner lists actual examples that qualify as school libraries.","A narrower concept always applies to more things than its broader parent.","Adding a qualifying attribute restricts, rather than enlarges, the class in this comparison.")],prerequisites=[8])

